<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Anton Chekhov</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1860">1860</a>–<a href="/years/1904">1904</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Anton Pavlovich Chekhov</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Anton Chekhov was a Russian playwright, short story writer and physician, born in Taganrog on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-of-azov">Sea of Azov</a>. He began writing stories to earn money and made formal innovations that influenced the modern short story. After the reception of The Seagull in 1896 he renounced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a>, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konstantin-stanislavski">Konstantin Stanislavski</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> Art Theatre revived the play to acclaim in 1898, then staged Uncle Vanya and premiered Three Sisters and The Cherry Orchard. With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henrik-ibsen">Henrik Ibsen</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/august-strindberg">August Strindberg</a> he is often counted among the three seminal figures of early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modernism">modernism</a> in the theatre. He died of tuberculosis at Badenweiler in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> in 1904, aged 44.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/anton-chekhov.jpg" alt="Anton Chekhov" width="330" height="467" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anton_Chekhov_with_bow-tie_sepia_image.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Medicine is my lawful wife and literature is my mistress. When I get tired of one I spend the night with the other.&#8221;</p><footer>Letter to Alexei Suvorin, 11 September 1888 &#183; tr. Constance Garnett, 1920 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/6408/pg6408-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;My holy of holies is the human body, health, intelligence, talent, inspiration, love, and the most absolute freedom—freedom from violence and lying, whatever forms they may take. This is the programme I would follow if I were a great artist.&#8221;</p><footer>Letter to Alexei Pleshcheyev &#183; tr. Constance Garnett, 1920 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/6408/pg6408-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;The artist must be not the judge of his characters and of their conversations, but merely an impartial witness.&#8221;</p><footer>Letter to Alexei Suvorin, May 1888 &#183; tr. Constance Garnett, 1920 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/6408/pg6408-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
